WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:17.392
Idea. You run a tuck shop where friends can pay later. At the start of the week, friends owed you twenty dollars. During the week they paid you fifty. At the end, they owe you thirty. How much did you sell on credit? Fifty, minus twenty, plus thirty: sixty dollars.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:28.626
Many small traders keep incomplete records: a bank summary, and lists of who owes what. We rebuild the missing figures, such as sales and purchases, from them.

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:46.132
Method. Picture a total trade receivables account. The opening balance is a debit. Receipts, discounts allowed and returns are credits. The closing balance goes in as balance carried down. The figure that makes it balance is credit sales.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:57.787
Purchases work the same way, in a trade payables account. Payments, discounts received, returns and the closing balance, minus the opening balance, give credit purchases.

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:04.941
If the question asks for total sales, add the cash sales. Total purchases add the cash purchases.

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:18.773
Worked example. Here is a real question from January 2023. Calculate Yamini's credit sales for the year ended thirtieth September 2022. Three marks.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:33.013
Trade receivables were twenty-one thousand four hundred on the first of October 2021, at the start of the year, and thirty-two thousand five hundred on the thirtieth of September 2022, at the end.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:40.328
The bank summary shows one hundred and sixty-seven thousand six hundred and fifty received from customers.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:48.877
Put them in the account. Opening receivables on the debit side. Receipts from the bank and the closing balance on the credit side.

00:01:49.450 --> 00:02:11.423
The credit side totals two hundred thousand one hundred and fifty. Take away the twenty-one thousand four hundred. Credit sales are one hundred and seventy-eight thousand seven hundred and fifty dollars. The thirty-two thousand five hundred is brought down as next year's opening debit. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:19.908
Common mistake. A common mistake is adding the opening balance. Those sales were made last year, so take them off.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:27.677
Another is forgetting discounts or returns. They also reduce what customers owe, so they go on the credit side.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:36.593
Your turn. Now you try. Find Kwame's credit purchases, then his total purchases. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:56.663
Answer. In the payables account, payments, discounts received and the closing balance are debits: fifty-six thousand eight hundred. Take away the opening six thousand two hundred. Credit purchases are fifty thousand six hundred. The closing seven thousand nine hundred comes down on the credit side.

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:04.363
Add the cash purchases of two thousand five hundred. Total purchases are fifty-three thousand one hundred dollars.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:13.534
Remember: picture the total account, enter what you know, and the balancing figure is the missing one. Now try the practice questions.
